abstract:
Let $f$ be a holomorphic cusp form for $SL(2,\mathbb{Z})$. In this paper, we prove for $T^{\frac{1}{3}} \ll M \ll T^{\frac{1}{2}}$, the following short interval second moment
  \begin{equation*}
  \int_T^{T+M}\left|L\left(\frac12+it,f\right)
  \right|^2 dt \ll_{f,ε}
  T^ε\left( M + \frac{T}{M}+\sqrt{TM}\right).
  \end{equation*}
  The proof uses the trivial delta method along with conductor lowering technique.

Within the Lorentz--Schatten scale, we identify the sharp dimension-free endpoint of the antisymmetric Lévy area for a nondominated family of continuous Hilbert-valued semimartingale laws with bounded drift and uniformly trace-bounded bracket density. A causal dyadic construction supplies one quasi-sure second-order Itô selector. Writing $A=\operatorname{Anti}(\mathbb X^I)$, for every finite $r$, \[
  \sup_P\left\|\|A^P_{s,t}\|_{\mathcal S_{1,\infty}}\right\|_{L^r(P)}
  \le C_{r,Λ,B,T}|t-s|. \] A Pfaffian--exterior identity and factorial decay of alternating Itô iterates imply the critical $N^{-1}$ singular-value scale. For every fixed $t\in(0,T]$, one explicit Gaussian martingale law satisfies \[
  A_{0,t}\in\mathcal S_{1,\infty}
  \setminus\bigcup_{1\leρ<\infty}\mathcal S_{1,ρ}
  \qquad\text{a.s.}, \] so no finite Lorentz refinement contains the area universally. The remainder of the paper develops consequences of this spectral theorem. The covariance defect is trace class under every model, while bounded trace-sensitive responses distinguish its trace-class topology from every weaker Schatten or Lorentz--Schatten topology on the positive trace-bounded sector. Thus, within these scales, the area--covariance endpoint pair is $\mathcal S_{1,\infty}^{\rm skew}\times\mathcal S_1^{\rm sa}$. The finite-Schatten layers of its separable envelope carry a locally Lipschitz two-dual Itô calculus, and a single energy--circulation observable together with finite-dimensional Brownian supports realizes the resulting continuity obstruction.

We investigate the optimal Bianchi-Egnell-type quantitative stability constant for the critical nonlocal Sobolev inequality on the Heisenberg group $\mathbb{H}^{n}$, \begin{equation}\label{nS}
  S_{HL}(Q,μ)\left(\int_{\mathbb{H}^{n}}\int_{\mathbb{H}^{n}}
  \frac{|u(ξ)|^{Q^{\ast}_μ}|u(η)|^{Q^{\ast}_μ}}
  {|η^{-1}ξ|^μ}\,dξdη\right)^{\frac{1}{Q^{\ast}_μ}}
  \leq \int_{\mathbb{H}^{n}}|\nabla_{\mathbb{H}}u|^{2}dξ,
  \qquad u\in S^{1,2}(\mathbb{H}^{n}), \end{equation} where $Q=2n+2$, $n\geq1$, $0<μ<Q$, and $Q^{\ast}_μ=(2Q-μ)/(Q-2)$. Let $\mathfrak{M}$ denote the manifold of Jerison-Lee bubbles, and let $H_{NS}$ be the infimum of the quotient between the deficit in \eqref{nS} and $\mathrm{dist}(u,\mathfrak{M})^{2}$. The central issue is that the Euclidean nonlocal problem and the local Folland-Stein-Sobolev problem each possess their own spectral and compactness structures, whereas the present problem couples the HLS interaction with the noncommutative conformal geometry of $\mathbb{H}^{n}$.
  Under the stated conditions on $(n,μ)$, these strict thresholds, together with a Heisenberg-group profile decomposition and a nonlocal Br{e}zis-Lieb splitting, imply that $H_{NS}$ is attained. The minimizer then yields the strict comparison $H_{NS}>H_{BE}$ with the optimal stability constant for the local Folland-Stein-Sobolev inequality. We further prove that the sharp universal upper constant for the deficit-to-distance comparison is $1$ and characterize equality. Finally, for the associated Euler-Lagrange equation, we formulate the corresponding residual quotient and derive a strict single-bubble upper bound; this critical-point statement requires a separate expansion and does not follow from attainment of $H_{NS}$.
